# Line endings.
#
# This checkout runs with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites text files to CRLF
# on checkout. That is harmless for most of the tree and fatal for anything a
# Linux kernel has to execute: a shebang line ending in a carriage return makes
# the interpreter path `/usr/bin/env bash\r`, which does not exist, so the
# script fails to exec with a message naming an interpreter nobody wrote.
#
# It was found by the installer ISO's own guard rather than by a build, because
# the files were authored with LF and only became CRLF when a second worktree
# checked them out fresh. That is the worst shape for this kind of fault: it is
# invisible in the worktree that created it and appears in every clone after.
#
# Everything below is bound for Linux or for a container, so it is pinned to LF
# regardless of platform or user configuration.

*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.timer         text eol=lf
*.socket        text eol=lf
*.mount         text eol=lf

# Subiquity autoinstall answer files. They are read by cloud-init inside the
# installer, and they carry no extension to match on.
console/scripts/iso/user-data   text eol=lf
console/scripts/iso/meta-data   text eol=lf

# Batch files are the mirror case: a Windows shell can mis-parse an LF-only
# batch file, so these are pinned the other way rather than left to chance.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# Capture evidence. The completeness inventory records a SHA-256 for every capture and
# a contract test re-hashes the file on disk, so a normalising rewrite would not merely
# annoy someone -- it would invalidate the evidence and read as tampering. Git detects
# PNG as binary by itself; this states it, for the same reason the generated renderer is
# pinned rather than left to chance: an implicit rule is one nobody can see stop applying.
console/release/captures/**/*.png   binary

# Generated design-parity data. Both files are re-derived byte for byte by their
# generators and compared with a --check freshness gate wired into `npm test`. The
# generators write LF, so on a checkout that translates to CRLF the gate reports the
# file stale when nothing about it has changed -- proven here: identical content passed
# as LF and failed as CRLF, which would turn a fresh Windows clone red for a reason no
# diff can show. Pinning is what makes the gate mean what it says.
console/design-reference/*.generated.json   text eol=lf

# The website design reference. Its files are exported from the design tool with LF, so a
# checkout that translates line endings makes every one of them differ from a fresh export
# by exactly its own line count -- which reads as a changed design when nothing changed at
# all. Measured: all nine overlapping files were byte-identical once normalised.
design/site/**   text eol=lf
console/release/captures/ui-drive/**/*.png   binary

# The React runtime vendored for the design-parity capture harness. Its bytes are checked
# against the sha384 subresource-integrity hashes design/support.js itself declares, by
# `vendor-design-react-host.mjs --check`, which npm test runs. A checkout that translated its
# line endings would change those bytes and turn that gate red on a fresh Windows clone with
# nothing wrong with the file -- the same failure the generated parity data is pinned against,
# except here the mismatch reads as a tampered dependency rather than a stale generator.
console/design-reference/vendor/*   binary
